ut look this is we  decided early on that well this is a  movie that centers around one person it  has 20 main characters we want  to be as much from the point of view of  the people on the campaign the point of  view of his family the point of view of  the various journalists and it was  written on the shoulders of their  experience to start with what is  essentially a tale of a fulcrum moment  in American politics and to get it to  the screen there are great great  political stories out there but not all  of them get told in this fashion no I  think very few get told in this in this  fashion I mean I think what was it's  it's obviously you know the the he was  based on the book but the book is very  sprawling and as you know we talked  about it it's you know I covers a lot of  territory before and after the scandal I  think to adapt this you needed we needed  sort of a unity of creative vision and I  think you know the first time we all sat  down Jason and Jay and I we we all  wanted to tell the same story which is  not just the true story but a story that  was that showed the complexity of all  the people involved no heroes and  villains  she always says but but to have it be to  give everybody their perspective to make  a movie that wasn't going to hit people  over the head and tell them what to  think that was that was you know a big  challenge and we all wanted it to feel  super real and like you'd been dropped  into a world and hopefully you feel that  way where you know sometimes you have to  catch ups frankly sometimes it might be  a little confusing but but we're you  know you have the sense that you're  really dropped into something and that  that feels very authentic I think that  the fact that we were all in the same  creative page made adapting this not  only very doable but you know super fun  but between the two of us you know Matt  and I've done eight presidential  campaigns and we're often turned off by  you know a lot of what we the portrayals  of that world that we'd seen before  because you end up with one-dimensional  archetypal characters good guys and bad  guys black hats and white hats heroes  and villains and in all of our time in  in Washington which is a lot you know we  never met any through-and-through heroes  we never met any  maybe Metta through a few through a few  through and through villains but no it  was important it was important for us in  this not to as matt said not to have it  be a message movie but to have it feel  like the world that we spent so much  time in and that world is a world  populated by human beings whether  they're journalists or candidates or  voters or spouses or staffers they're  all human beings and I think humans make  the three-dimensional humans make the  best characters they're all human beings  who are in difficult spots usually under  a lot of pressure facing something that  they don't know how to do and they're  trying to do their best that it in this  case Matt had stumbled upon this period  where people were facing something for  the very first time this had never  happened before to any of them so we  watched these human being struggle in  that and it was great to have Jason  pushing us to go even further and deeper  into reality you know it was just it was  a really inspiring I really a joyous  experience from the very very first time  we sat down and start breaking story  Helen as one of the producers with Jason  and a couple of other folks was this a  hard sell was it oh god another politics  movie was it that was 30 years ago or  was it a natural well we were very lucky  we did Jason and I did tell this movie  telly with Aaron Gilbert and that sort  of partnership of the three of us for  that movie and I mean there were more  producers I went to but that for the  became a sort of way we had such an easy  way to talk about it creatively and talk  about the ideas and so we were given a  lot of support because it was you know I  think this amazing script t